WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.920
Safe arithmetic often means checking whether an operation is valid before performing it.

2
00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:09.462
Addition, subtraction, multiplication, division, shifts, and unit conversions can all have preconditions.

3
00:00:09.462 --> 00:00:17.031
If the result cannot be represented or does not make sense for the domain, the operation should not quietly continue.

4
00:00:17.031 --> 00:00:20.815
Signed overflow must not be treated as acceptable program behavior.

5
00:00:20.815 --> 00:00:25.357
It can invalidate assumptions that the optimizer, reviewer, and maintainer rely on.

6
00:00:25.357 --> 00:00:33.305
A defensive design checks maximum and minimum boundaries before performing an operation that could exceed them, especially when the result controls

7
00:00:33.305 --> 00:00:34.818
allocation, indexing, or limits.

8
00:00:34.818 --> 00:00:41.252
Unsigned arithmetic has defined wraparound behavior, but that does not automatically make it safe for application logic.

9
00:00:41.252 --> 00:00:51.471
A counter that wraps, a subtraction that moves from zero to a large value, or a size calculation that rolls over can still produce a wrong decision.

10
00:00:51.471 --> 00:00:55.255
Defined arithmetic is not the same as domain-valid arithmetic.

11
00:00:55.255 --> 00:00:57.526
Subtraction and multiplication deserve special care.

12
00:00:57.526 --> 00:01:02.446
Subtraction can underflow when a value is already at or below a boundary.

13
00:01:02.446 --> 00:01:11.529
Multiplication is common in size calculations, such as element count times element size, and it can exceed the type range before any allocation or

14
00:01:11.529 --> 00:01:12.286
copy happens.

15
00:01:12.286 --> 00:01:16.828
Division also has edge cases, including zero divisors and minimum signed values.

16
00:01:16.828 --> 00:01:21.369
Functions should define what happens when a result cannot be represented safely.

17
00:01:21.369 --> 00:01:27.803
The policy might be an error return, an optional result, an exception, or rejecting the input earlier.

18
00:01:27.803 --> 00:01:29.317
What matters is consistency.

19
00:01:29.317 --> 00:01:33.858
Silent truncation, wraparound, or partial progress should not be the accidental behavior.

20
00:01:33.858 --> 00:01:36.129
Testing should include near-limit arithmetic.

21
00:01:36.129 --> 00:01:44.077
Values around zero, one, maximums, minimums, expected domain caps, and unit conversion boundaries are more revealing than ordinary middle-of-range

22
00:01:44.077 --> 00:01:44.455
values.

23
00:01:44.455 --> 00:01:48.240
Good tests verify both successful calculations and safe failure paths.
